WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.500
Model intake should be a repeatable workflow, not a one-off download by whoever needs a model fastest.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:16.120
The purpose of intake is to move a checkpoint from unknown or partially known status into a controlled decision path where evidence can be reviewed before shared use.

3
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.920
New checkpoints should start in a controlled location.

4
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:26.180
That location may be an intake bucket, restricted registry, review workspace, or staging environment.

5
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:34.520
The key is that the artifact is not immediately available to production services, broad user groups, or automated deployment jobs before review is complete.

6
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:50.080
A practical intake workflow reviews source, documentation, license terms, intended use, file format, companion files, integrity evidence, dependency expectations, conversion needs, and storage location.

7
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:56.580
If signatures, attestations, or registry metadata are available, they should be captured in the model record.

8
00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.100
Evaluation is part of intake.

9
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.880
The team should confirm that the model is suitable for the intended workflow and does not break known requirements.

10
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:14.020
That includes task quality, output format, latency, resource needs, policy fit, and documented limitations.

11
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.920
Not every model needs the same depth of review, but the depth should match the risk.

12
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:26.960
Approval should record who accepted the model, what evidence was reviewed, what limitations apply, and where the model is allowed to run.

13
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:36.020
A simple checklist can prevent confusion without slowing teams more than necessary, especially when it separates experimental use from shared and production use.
